[agent:result]         When the Hopsworks scheduler fires a job, it injects `HOPS_START_TIME`
[agent:result]         and `HOPS_END_TIME` env vars describing the data interval the run
[agent:result]         should process. If `start_time` / `end_time` are not passed to `read`,
[agent:result]         these env vars are used as defaults, so the same feature-pipeline
[agent:result]         code works whether launched by the scheduler, by a backfill
[agent:result]         (`Job.run(start_time=..., end_time=...)`), or manually:
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]         ```python
[agent:result]         # No explicit time args — falls back to HOPS_START_TIME / HOPS_END_TIME
[agent:result]         # (scheduler-supplied) if set, otherwise reads the whole feature group.
[agent:result]         fg.read()
[agent:result]         ```
[agent:result] 
[agent:result]     Parameters:
[agent:result]         wallclock_time:
[agent:result]             If specified, retrieves feature group as of specific point in time.
[agent:result]             If not specified, returns as of most recent time.
[agent:result]             Strings should be formatted in one of the following formats `%Y-%m-%d`, `%Y-%m-%d %H`, `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M`, `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S`, or `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S.%f`.
[agent:result]             Mutually exclusive with `start_time` / `end_time` (time-travel vs
[agent:result]             event-time filter are different operations). An explicit `wallclock_time`
[agent:result]             takes precedence over the scheduler-injected `HOPS_START_TIME` /
[agent:result]             `HOPS_END_TIME` env vars: when it is set, those defaults are ignored.
